{
  "query": "Your team runs a multiregional news subscription platform on Cloud Spanner. You have deployed five read-only replicas across three continents to lower read latency for users worldwide. You want to follow Google best practices so users get the fastest reads from nearby regions. How should the application be configured?",
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Configure an external HTTP(S) global load balancer to distribute read traffic across the replicas",
      "explanation": "A global HTTP(S) load balancer routes HTTP traffic to backends across regions and is typically used for web endpoints rather than database replica selection",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Program the application to select the geographically closest read-only replica based on the user&apos;s location",
      "explanation": "The application uses client geolocation or region awareness to pick the nearest read-only replica to help reduce round-trip time",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Use Cloud DNS geo-location policies to steer read requests to regional endpoints",
      "explanation": "Cloud DNS can return geo-based answers so that different users receive region-specific DNS responses",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Randomly choose any available read replica for each read call to spread load evenly",
      "explanation": "Random selection distributes requests but does not consider proximity or latency for users in different regions",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "answer": "<p>The correct option is <b>Program the application to select the geographically closest read-only replica based on the user&apos;s location</b>.</p><p>Cloud Spanner is a managed service that already exposes a single service endpoint and internally replicates data across regions. The way to achieve the lowest read latency is to have the client route read requests to the nearest read replica. You can implement this by using the Spanner client library features that select the closest replica or by adding simple application logic that chooses the nearest location based on the user. This aligns with Google guidance to take advantage of read replicas for locality and to keep reads near users.</p><p><i>Configure an external HTTP(S) global load balancer to distribute read traffic across the replicas</i> is incorrect because Spanner is not a service you place behind your own load balancer. The service manages request routing and replica selection and the client connects to the Spanner API rather than to backends you control.</p><p><i>Use Cloud DNS geo-location policies to steer read requests to regional endpoints</i> is incorrect because you do not manage per-replica DNS endpoints for Spanner. The service exposes a single endpoint and DNS geo policies cannot target individual Spanner replicas.</p><p><i>Randomly choose any available read replica for each read call to spread load evenly</i> is incorrect because random selection can send traffic to distant replicas and increase latency. Best practice is to route reads to the nearest replica for predictable low latency.</p>",
